# Fetch-heavy retrieval: does this page repeat what the agent already has? (16 cases).
#
# redundant_page: purpose + a digest of what the agent already holds + the page -> drop | keep.
#   This is the lever the end-to-end A/B could not exercise, because its agent fetched one
#   or two documents per task (benchmarks/ab, docs/savings.md). It only bites when a job
#   gathers many sources about the same event, which is what a dossier does.
#
#   The hard cases are the ones that decide whether this point is usable at all:
#   - the same figures attributed to a different source, when the purpose is corroboration
#     (keep: the attribution is the new information);
#   - a page that restates what is known and adds one qualification (keep);
#   - a wire story reprinted under another masthead (drop);
#   - a page about the same event that answers a different part of the purpose (keep);
#   - a page with nothing to do with the purpose, which also adds nothing and is also
#     dropped (rd-15). The overlap with page triage is real and the label says so.
#
# RELABELLED after the first run, on the question's own criteria and not because the
# model disagreed: rd-04 adds the governor's announced visit and rd-12 adds the sector
# and the company's status, neither of which the `known` digest carries. The original
# labels were the annotator drifting from 'adds nothing at all' to 'same topic'. Both
# numbers are reported in docs/results/2026-09-24-new-points/summary.md.
#
# Single-annotator, authored 2026-09-24. Content is Spanish without accents.
